A `scope` lazy variadic array keeps its arguments' frames on the stack

The arguments of a $(LINK2 $(ROOT_DIR)spec/function.html#lazy_variadic_functions,
lazy variadic function) are delegates. When the array of delegates is declared
`scope`, the caller no longer allocates a GC closure for the variables they
read, so such calls work in `@nogc` code and with `-betterC`:

---
int sum(scope int delegate() @nogc[] xs...) @nogc
{
    int s;
    foreach (x; xs)
        s += x();
    return s;
}

int g(int a, int b) @nogc => sum(a, b + 1); // previously: allocates closure for `g()`
---

A declared `scope` on such an array now covers its delegates too, and with
`-preview=dip1000` a `@safe` function may not let them escape: storing an
element (`global = xs[0]`), returning one, appending the array to another
(`arr ~= xs`, `xs ~ ys`, `arr[] = xs[]`), or passing the array to a typesafe
variadic parameter that is not declared `scope` are errors. An override may not
drop the `scope`.

The `scope` every typesafe variadic array gets implicitly covers only the
array, as before: without a declared `scope`, the delegates may escape and the
caller allocates a closure. A declared `scope` on a typesafe variadic parameter
is part of the function's mangled name again, as it was before 2.101.
